    <item>
      <title>CoreWeave’s Bond Rout Should Make the Fed Cautious</title>
      <link>https://www.bloomberg.com/opinion/articles/2026-10-20/coreweave-s-bond-rout-should-make-the-fed-cautious</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">438c7d49817fe9d91eeb4c68</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:00:12 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>Corporate credit, monetary policy’s sleeping giant, is finally stirring. Cloud computing service provider CoreWeave Inc.’s bonds dropped this month, sending yields on its 2032 notes as high as 15%. Yields for junk and investment-grade debt reached the highest since 2025 and 2023, respectively. The implication is that the cost of corporate credit, a key channel through which central banks tap the economy’s brakes to cool inflation, may start to weigh on growth.</description>
      <dc:creator>Jonathan Levin</dc:creator>
